<!--
web/index.html — required changes for YouTube iframes on Flutter Web
=====================================================================

PROBLEM 1: Content-Security-Policy (CSP)
─────────────────────────────────────────
If your index.html has a <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"> tag
that doesn't include YouTube in frame-src, the browser silently blocks
every iframe. No Dart error is thrown — the player just shows blank.

Add or update the CSP meta tag to include the origins below.
If you have NO CSP tag, you don't need to add one — browsers are
permissive by default.

REPLACE your existing CSP meta tag (if any) with:
-->

<meta
http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy"
content="
default-src 'self';
script-src  'self' 'unsafe-inline' 'unsafe-eval'
https://www.youtube.com
https://s.ytimg.com;
frame-src   https://www.youtube.com
https://www.youtube-nocookie.com;
img-src     'self' data: blob:
https://*.ytimg.com
https://*.ggpht.com;
style-src   'self' 'unsafe-inline';
connect-src 'self'
https://smartschoolserver.ramchintech.com;
"
/>

<!--
PROBLEM 2: CORS on your API server
────────────────────────────────────
Flutter Web runs in a browser. When it calls your API at
https://smartschoolserver.ramchintech.com the browser enforces CORS.
If the server doesn't return Access-Control-Allow-Origin the browser
blocks the response silently. Dart's catch block receives a JS TypeError,
not a useful exception — so _hasError is set with no useful message.

On your Express/Node backend, add:

const cors = require('cors');
app.use(cors({
origin: '*',           // or your Flutter Web domain in production
methods: ['GET'],
}));

Or if you're not using Express, add the header manually:

res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Origin', '*');
res.header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods', 'GET, OPTIONS');

PROBLEM 3: Mixed content (HTTP vs HTTPS)
─────────────────────────────────────────
If your Flutter Web app is served over HTTPS but your API is HTTP,
the browser blocks it as mixed content. Ensure your API is also HTTPS.
Your current baseUrl uses https:// so this should be fine already.
-->